            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "LifeSmart is a consumer-products brand that also sells space heaters and massage chairs. Its treadmill store and support point to Supply Chain Sources (SCS), described by a retailer as based in Allen, Texas. It is not LifeSpan Fitness, and not the Chinese smart-home company of the same name.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What are the LifeSmart TM6500 specs?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Costco lists a 4.0 HP brushless motor, up to 14 mph, a 20 by 60-inch belt, a 325 lb capacity, 231 lb, a 22-inch pivoting touchscreen and 30 programs. Its page gives two incline figures, 10% with -3% decline and 15%. It is sold online at Costco only.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is LifeSmart PowerTouch?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "A motorised folding system that raises and lowers the deck at the press of a button, with sensors that LifeSmart says stop it lowering if something is underneath. It is the main reason to choose a LifeSmart over a conventional folding treadmill.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is the LifeSmart TM3000 a good treadmill?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For walking, it is adequate: LifeSmart lists 0 to 12.5 mph, 10% incline, -3% decline, a 20 by 55-inch belt and 300 lb, with PowerTouch folding, at $999. But its belt is short for running, its motor figures conflict, and its warranty is one year. Rivals at $999 offer longer decks and warranties.",
