        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>Is the LifeSmart TM4500 discontinued?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Yes. On 5 November 2026 LifeSmart’s product page said the TM4500 has been discontinued and will not be restocked, and recommended the TM4000C, with the same 3.5 HP brushless motor, 0 to 14 mph, 10% incline, -3% decline, 20 by 60-inch belt and PowerTouch fold, at $1,199.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What warranty do LifeSmart treadmills have?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              One year. LifeSmart’s warranty page states a 1-year limited warranty on all products, covering functional parts for personal use, with claims made through the retailer where you bought it. Extended plans were offered at checkout from $49.99 to $99.99.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Who makes LifeSmart treadmills?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              LifeSmart is a consumer-products brand that also sells space heaters and massage chairs. Its treadmill store and support point to Supply Chain Sources (SCS), described by a retailer as based in Allen, Texas. It is not LifeSpan Fitness, and not the Chinese smart-home company of the same name.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What are the LifeSmart TM6500 specs?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Costco lists a 4.0 HP brushless motor, up to 14 mph, a 20 by 60-inch belt, a 325 lb capacity, 231 lb, a 22-inch pivoting touchscreen and 30 programs. Its page gives two incline figures, 10% with -3% decline and 15%. It is sold online at Costco only.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What is LifeSmart PowerTouch?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              A motorised folding system that raises and lowers the deck at the press of a button, with sensors that LifeSmart says stop it lowering if something is underneath. It is the main reason to choose a LifeSmart over a conventional folding treadmill.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is the LifeSmart TM3000 a good treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              For walking, it is adequate: LifeSmart lists 0 to 12.5 mph, 10% incline, -3% decline, a 20 by 55-inch belt and 300 lb, with PowerTouch folding, at $999. But its belt is short for running, its motor figures conflict, and its warranty is one year. Rivals at $999 offer longer decks and warranties.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Where can I buy a LifeSmart treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              LifeSmart’s own online store, Dick’s Sporting Goods (TM3000 and TM4000) and Costco, which sells its own models such as the TM6500 and TM55 online and has carried the TM4800 in warehouses. Costco’s returns policy is a real advantage given the one-year warranty.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

        <!-- Why trust us -->
        <section id="why-trust-us" class="ed-trust not-prose">
          <h2>Why trust Treadmill Reviews USA?</h2>
          <p>
            Every recommendation is checked against the numbers that matter for a treadmill: the maker's own
            listing and manual, at least one independent source, the warranty terms and the CPSC recall
            database. Where sources disagree we say so rather than pick a number, we say plainly when a
            machine has not been tested hands-on, and we never accept payment for a placement. <a href="/editorial-standards/">Read our review process.</a>
          </p>
